    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the codebase's established conventions.\n\n- **Right unit, right package.** `OwnerCheckDigit` is a pure, stateless derivation helper in `rest.function.owner`, matching the existing family of `Owner<Thing>` helpers (`OwnerIdentity`, `OwnerRegion`, `OwnerHousehold`, `OwnerTelephone`, `OwnerEmail`). A maintainer would expect a derived owner value to live exactly here.\n- **Mirrors the closest precedent.** The wiring is a near-exact parallel of `identityKey`: a dedicated helper plus a thin `@Named` default in `OwnerMapper` delegating to a `static checkDigit(Owner)`. The two-method split (`checkDigit(Owner)` reading the stored field + a lower-level `luhn(String)` over raw input) echoes `OwnerIdentity.identityKey(Owner)` / `identityKey(parts)`, so it reads as idiomatic rather than novel.\n- **No catch-all growth, no duplication.** The mapper gains one small, consistent delegating method rather than inline algorithm logic; the Luhn computation is isolated in its own class. Keeping it a read-time derivation (rather than folding it into `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`) is the correct call \u2014 it's a function of the already-stored `customerCode`, not part of code assignment, and the Javadoc states that rationale.\n- The OpenAPI addition (`readOnly`, `0\u20139`, int32) is consistent with the sibling derived fields.\n\nNo structural improvements warranted.",
